Two corrections to my record: (i) "`refusal(ERROR: lines): 0` proves no ERROR lines" was an over-reading -- what the preserved launcher output proves is that stderr contributed 0 WALL-pattern matches (`wall(ERROR: + stdout): 1` and the answer's stdout alone matches exactly 1, measured), which is what the empty-`.err` fixture reproduces; (ii) my first verdict-less fixture contained the word "verdict" ("No verdict line here"), so cxlaunch's case-insensitive `grep -ic 'VERDICT'` counted 1 and Option A never consulted stdout -- a fixture defect, caught because A and B printed the same answer, repaired, and the receipt records both runs. Also recorded: the first Option-A build lost one backslash level on the heredoc path (`\b` -> nothing), exactly the carrier's chr(92) warning; rebuilt with `chr(92)`. Astra's note that `:41` ("stdout ... may be grepped freely") is the premise being corrected, not a supporter of B, is right -- B is argued by `:40` and by the measurement, and `:41` should be rewritten with the fix. OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md:24716 and `ledger.json:62489` prescribe A: correct both to B (L4).

## v2.3 `dashokclass` -- CORRECTED

- Astra's three counterexamples VERIFIED at HEAD: `ledger.json:30463` `"d": "OK"` with `:30467` `*** SEAT FAILURE -- QUOTA WALL, not agreement. ***` (widthkillclass, assess, agy, grade MANUAL, outcome UNVERIFIED); `:13378` wdcnoirq review codex `*** UNFINISHED -- investigation without conclusion ***`; `:27711` wdcfloodstale research codex `INVESTIGATION WITHOUT CONCLUSION`, grade UNKNOWN. The all-439 sweep is WITHDRAWN. Partition (`py/okpartition2.py` over HEAD's ledger): grades CONFIRMED 323 / MANUAL 95 / none 18 / UNKNOWN 3; a strict marker set (SEAT FAILURE, QUOTA WALL, UNFINISHED, without conclusion, no verdict line, WALLED, NOT FIRED, SEAT DOWN, defanged hold markers) hits 43 notes, of which by reading at least SIX are not an approval or an answer at all (`widthkillclass/assess/agy` quota wall; `wdcnoirq/review/codex` unfinished; `wdcfloodstale/research/codex` without conclusion; `wdcdataoutdrain/research/kimi` "BOTH TRANSPORTS" failed, substitute stalled at 127 bytes; `sgideprimop/assess/codex` a defanged hold marker, a record; `wdcatapistswap/review/glm` down on its first firing, answered on refire -- an answer, but its note must say which), 15 are format-miss ANSWERS, 7 are covered-by-substitute kimi answers, and the rest are notes that merely discuss seat failure. **Mechanically safe sweep subset: 262 entries** (grade CONFIRMED, no strict marker, outcome not UNVERIFIED/PARTIAL/HOLD/NOT-READY); **177 owe a per-entry read** before any glyph is written. The sweep ships as `OK -> �o"` for the 262 plus whatever the read assigns to the 177 (`�?�` for the six above), never as one rule.
- Two claims of mine withdrawn as FALSE by reading `gen_dashboard.py`: holds do NOT render pend -- `:140-146` route a semantic hold (`st.live_holds`/`st.is_held`) to `_held_cell`, `:129-130` emit `class="c hold"` and �?, -- and an unknown `d` keeps its own TEXT in the cell (`:178` prints `{d}`) with class pend; so "HELD/WALL/NOT-FIRED render the same glyph" was wrong in both halves. And "the fixture would be vacuous under the sweep" is FALSE: `run_controls.py:45-46` builds fixture entries with an explicit `d` and `:160-161` replaces the ledger's rows with `ROWS`, so a real-ledger sweep cannot touch `t_okclass`'s `d="OK"`; agy's DASH row stays a discriminating control for a MAPPING fix (which is still rejected) and is the wrong control for a sweep.
- "~1,000 free-text tokens" -> **399** (Astra): 3,170 explicit `d` values minus 1,773 glyphs minus the named categories (OK 439, N/A 253, y 89, dates 128, HELD 31, NOT-FIRED 22, WALL 7, blank 29) = 399; the strip sums to 3,753 of 5,150 because its `�?�` count already includes the 1,980 missing-`d` defaults (`:261`).
- The domain-check RECOMMENDATION stands, with Astra's completeness conditions adopted: it must (i) treat a missing `d` as the existing default (`�?�`), (ii) pass entries that `stage_tokens` classifies as annotations/holds/records whatever their `d`, (iii) state the cutoff inclusively and test before/on/after it, and (iv) carry a positive control that a VALID post-cutoff glyph entry passes (the existing DASH fixtures are dated 2026-09-10..12 and cannot test future acceptance) beside the negative (post-cutoff off-domain -> exit 2) and the mutant (cutoff test removed). The "pre-cutoff allowance" sweep assertion I proposed is withdrawn: it admits the unchanged ledger. Its replacement is `("LEDGER no OK remains", count of d == "OK" == 0)` after the sweep, paired with `("LEDGER six stay non-approval", the six entries above carry �?� not �o")`.

## v2.4 The agy launcher gate -- CORRECTED; receipt `launcherctl/ctl_receipt_v2.txt`

The gate line was extracted by `grep` from each launcher version (frozen 20:31, fixed 21:11, current 22:06 -- the thin-stdout form that shipped after my packet, with its durable receipt `regress/artifacts/agypack_20261013/launcher_controls_20261013.txt` written 22:06:56; my packet's "no receipt anywhere" was true when written and must not be read as a present-state claim) and a PROPOSED form, and run on six fixtures:

| fixture | want | FROZEN | FIXED (21:11) | CURRENT (22:06) | **PROPOSED** |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| C1 real bad-model stderr text, empty stdout | RED | RED | RED | RED | **RED** |
| C2 the 14,387-byte answer quoting the pattern at :218, empty stderr | GREEN | RED | GREEN | GREEN | **GREEN** |
| C3 thin stdout (20 b) carrying the error, empty stderr (HARVEST's control 3) | -- | RED | RED | RED | GREEN -- withdrawn as a control, see below |
| C4 full answer whose VERDICT line quotes the pattern | GREEN | RED | RED | GREEN | **GREEN** |
| C5 84-byte healthy answer quoting the pattern (Astra's case) | GREEN | RED | RED | **RED** | **GREEN** |
| C6 pre-flight: bogus model name `gemini-9-nope` against `agy models` | RED | -- | -- | -- | **RED** |
| C7 pre-flight: the real model name | GREEN | -- | -- | -- | **GREEN** |

PROPOSED = `grep -qiE 'unknown model|invalid model|unknown effort|invalid effort|model .* not found' "$ERR"` (stderr ONLY, stdout never consulted) plus, before launch, `agy models | cut -f1 | grep -qxF "$MODEL"` -> exit 5. Its warrant is measured rather than argued: `agyprobe/` shows `agy --model gemini-9-nope` exits 1 with **0 bytes on stdout** and `error: invalid model selection (--model "gemini-9-nope" --effort "high"): ...` on stderr, and a bogus `--effort ultrahigh` likewise (`invalid --effort "ultrahigh" (valid: low, medium, high, xhigh, max)`), so a bad name never reaches stdout and C3 is a fixture of a state the tool does not produce -- which is why it is withdrawn, not merely re-graded. C5 settles Astra's objection 3: the CURRENT line fails it, so the thin-stdout remedy I proposed is withdrawn (size is not content; the project already has a 712-byte substantive answer on record), and the current launcher owes this edit with C1/C2/C4/C5/C6/C7 as its controls. Note the pattern `invalid effort` does not match agy's real text `invalid --effort`; the real message still trips `invalid model selection`, and the pre-flight makes the post-run grep a second line of defence rather than the gate.

My earlier receipt (`launcherctl/ctl_receipt.txt`) holds only C4 because the `tee` captured the last run; Astra read that correctly. The complete re-run is `ctl_receipt_v2.txt`.

## v2.5 The "closing overrulings"

1. HARVEST item 1 ("codex refusals scored answered twice ... :480, :486-488"). The LINE half stands (Astra agrees: `:365`, `:475-477`, `:530`). The "twice" half is WITHDRAWN as an error of that item: it is scoped to the two #563 refusals and is true in scope. What stands is a FILING fact for `seatverdictrefusal`: the class is eight invocations (fifteen files with copies, nine byte-distinct versions) since 2026-09-07, listed in v2.1, and the filing should carry them with the terminal-diagnostic remedy.
2. "deepseek 712 b ... (cosmetic)". Stands (Astra agrees), with two corrections to my record: `panel.sh:459` returns AFTER the `:455-457` error-content test, not "before any content test"; and the remedy must also cover the seat tally at `:708` (`-ge 800` -> `ok`) and must not credit an echoed brief, whose `VERDICT:` tokens `:468-470` already warns about -- the short-file branch therefore tests for a verdict token AND the absence of the brief's marker, with the 712-byte deepseek file (GREEN) and a short echoed-brief-plus-error file (RED) as its pair.
3. The launcher. Stands in substance (Astra agrees the C4 counterexample is real); its remedy is corrected as in v2.4, its receipt claim corrected as above.
Net: three HARVEST dispositions remain wrong, item 1 only in its line citations.

## v2.6 Corrections to my own packet, in place (L4)
- armthumbabortkill F2-F6 summary: "no `exit(`/`abort(` reachable from Thumb" is FALSE as compressed. agy's own A1.4 says, and HEAD confirms, that `memory_arm.c:79` (`case 2: ... exit(1)`, DACR value 2) and `:254` (`default: fatal("TODO: descriptor ...") exit(1)`, descriptor type 3) ARE reachable from any Thumb memory access with the MMU on -- rows `armdacr2kill` and `armdesc3kill`. What agy's flag 2 established is narrower: no `exit(` in `cpu_arm_instr.c`/`cpu_arm.c` is reachable from Thumb, the seven coprocessor exits are unreachable in Thumb state, and the five `armthumbabortkill` sites are `cpu->running = 0; return 0;` halts.
- A0: the three stderr sizes are 408, 409, 409; the adjudication jobs are the last TWO rows of `queue.relaunch.tsv` (`:27-28`; all four at `:25-28`); "re-fire at ~01:30" is the runner's recorded intent, not an event (as of 22:35 none of the ten has landed); the carrier copy is 263 added / 21 deleted lines ahead of HEAD (242 net), "284" was the changed-line count; the pass-1 copy's 21:06:08 is its CreationTime, its LastWriteTime is 2026-10-09 15:19:48.
- c563_seats F3: the 284-byte wall is in 18 canonical scratch invocations and 37 files at this census, not "seven panels / 34 copies".
- c563_seats F6: "the standing rule (a fallback-model ruling is a seat failure)" is narrowed to what the files say -- `RULING_PROVENANCE.txt:1-2` records this fallback as a seat failure and credits v2; the carrier (`:589-591`, `:814`) permits adjudication to degrade to the main loop and a substitute transport to cover a seat, so there is no blanket rule.
- A3: "each src/ and regress/ edit lands byte-identical in est/, build/, build-asan/ and /tmp/gxsec-build" -- the build-tree propagation and the two-part clearance apply to `src/*` (`precommit_check.sh:1022-1024`, `:1050`); the `regress/` edits owe est/ byte-identity under the shared-file rule only. The dirty `cpu_alpha_palcode.c` is 1,276 bytes longer than HEAD (my "20,502 / 21,778" were `str.count("")` artefacts, off by one each). "or exempt" is withdrawn: precommit E (`:1251-1255`) requires the identity guard of a touched differential and no exemption exists; the five diff_ieee_store.c edits ship with the guard or wait.
- A4: ROUTE PARITY -- the bound I proposed is kept and Astra's R8 clause is added: "rows are owed per enumerated mutant; two routes no enumerated mutant distinguishes collapse into one row". Label clause -- "only multi-way dispatch" is WITHDRAWN as the test; what stands is: relabelling cannot remove a dominating `if`'s floor obligation (DEPENDS-ON, `:1362-1365`), and label status is decided by the committed `:1417` test -- the selector flows into a body-read lvalue or is re-tested after the join -- not by syntax or arity, so a two-valued joined dispatch can qualify.
- stagetokens: after the `t_reread` re-pin, `X_filing_is_zero`'s expected score is 48/49 (it reds `CSP t_reread one-seat filing`); the archived 49/49 is the pre-re-pin value (Astra measured it in memory; it follows from `check_stage_panels.py:274` printing the filed line).
- c563_seats F7's "mtime" wording, and the `_scratchpad/panel_*` sweep in N3 (which excluded artifact copies and contradicted N1): both replaced by v2.1's population sweep.

Receipts added beside the earlier ones under `recd/adj_20261013/`: `v2/{build_variants.py,population_run.py,sv_*.py,classes_*.tsv,changes_head_to_*.txt}`, `v3/{build_v3.py (in v2/),population_run3.py (in v2/),classes_sv_v3.tsv,classes_sv_v3b.tsv,changes_head_to_sv_v3.txt,changes_head_to_sv_v3b.txt,census_files.txt,census_sentence.txt,census_anchored.txt}`, `agyprobe/{models.out,bogus.out,bogus.err,bogus2.err,probe_time.txt}`, `launcherctl/ctl_receipt_v2.txt`, `cxctl/{block_optA.sh,ctl_receipt_cxlaunch_v2.txt}`, `py/{okpartition.py,okpartition2.py}`. All volatile; copy into `regress/artifacts/` before any record cites them. Nothing was written to any project tree.


